  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm low-level thicknesses and\ndewpoints beneath strong ridging aloft will continue to keep \ntemperatures well above normal with potential record breaking \nheat continuing through Saturday, with heat index values \npeaking at 105-110 deg today for most of the area, and possibly\nagain for srn zones on Saturday.\n\nToday continues to have the strongest signal for the peak of \nthis week's heat wave, with highs ranging 95-100 for the \ninterior coastal plain, with low 90s coast. Thermal gradient \nshould be strong enough to allow for fast seabreeze progression \nand thus preclude heat indices reaching 105 for portions of the \nimmediate coast. The combo of hot temps well above 90 inland \ncoupled with TD's well into the 70s bring heat indices 105+ once\nagain inland. Some locales may once again flirt with vals above\n110 again, but not long enough or widespread for issuance of \nheat warnings. HREF probs remain below 20%. \n\nSaturday a cold front will begin sinking southward through the\narea, but at the same time TD vals inc with moisture gradient \ntightening ahead of it, and with temps fcst to reach into the \nmid to possibly upper 90s in swrn zones, another day of heat \nindices 105-110 degrees is possible before expected numerous \nshowers and storms ends the heat later in the day. Best chances \nto reach criteria are generally along and s of Hwy 70.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong upper level trough will develop off \nthe U.S. East Coast over the weekend, and this will help drive a\nstrong cold front south through the Carolinas on Saturday and\nSaturday night. Strong heating of a moist boundary layer ahead \nof the front should allow strong instability to develop from the\nMid-Atlantic south into North Carolina today. While some \nresidual subsidence/capping may be present beneath the ridge, \nespecially across NC, guidance show a decent signal for \nconvection developing upstream of ENC, and making a run towards \nthe nrn zones late this afternoon and evening. If convection \ncan arrive early enough, severe potential is there, esp north of\nHwy 70 and best chances north of Hwy 264. CAMs have generally \ninc in covg here. Instability is above the climo max for the \nfirst week of September, and deep layer shear above 25 kt. Main\nthreat will be potential for damaging wind gusts. \n\nOn Saturday, the ridge will continue to get suppressed further \nsouth and west with less influence across the Carolinas. This \nplus increased forcing along the cold front should support an \nimproved risk of deep convection, and less limiting factors, \ncompared to Friday. PW's/TD's inc further and with lowering hts\naloft, instability will be very strong. In fact, vals will be\nwell above the avg max for first week of September according to\nthe KMHX sounding climatology, with ML CAPE vals advertised by \nthe 03/12Z RRFS/ECM/NAM3k reaching or exceeding 4,000 J/KG! If\nconvective timing aligns with fropa/shortwave passage in the\nafternoon, a significant severe weather signal may be met as\nthis aligns with deep layer shear of 35-45kt. Machine learning\nsevere probs for damaging wind have increased for parts of ENC. \nIn addition to the severe threat, will have to watch for \npotential flash flood threat, as PW's surge to 2.25\" or higher, \nand with the expected numerous convection, very heavy rain rates\nare possible with deeper convection and lend to instances of \nflash flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Behind the front, temperatures and humidity \nwill finally get knocked down closer to normal for early \nSeptember, with more comfortable conditions lasting into early \nnext week. Still some residual moisture lingering on Sunday and\nmay see some sct showers, but overall trend is for drier esp as\nwe begin the work week with pops less than 20% lasting through\nmid week. Highs in the mid 80s with lows in the 60s is fcst and\nis where our temps should be for the first half of September.",
